#!/usr/bin/env perl

=head1 NAME

zfs-stats - LibreNMS JSON SNMP extend for gathering backups for ZFS

=head1 VERSION

0.4.0

=head1 DESCRIPTION

For more information, see L<https://docs.librenms.org/Extensions/Applications/#zfs>.

=head1 WHY "zfs-stats" AND NOT "zfs"

This script is deliberately not installed under the filename C<zfs>.
LibreNMS's usual extend-script convention names the on-disk file the
same as the repo filename, which would put a file literally called
C<zfs> on C<$PATH> (e.g. C</usr/local/bin/zfs> or C</etc/snmp/zfs>).
On any host where that directory precedes C</sbin>/C</usr/sbin> in
C<$PATH> -- common for C</usr/local/bin> -- running C<zfs list> or
C<zfs set> at a shell would silently execute this monitoring script
instead of the real OpenZFS C<zfs> binary. Naming the on-disk file
C<zfs-stats> avoids that collision. This is purely about the filename
on disk -- the SNMP-visible extend name configured in C<snmpd.conf>
(see L</SNMPD SETUP EXAMPLES>) is unrelated and does not need to
change.

=head1 SWITCHES

=head2 -p

Pretty print the JSON. If used with -b, this switch will be ignored.

=head2 -b

Gzip the output and convert to Base64.

=head2 -s

Include the full information for `zpool status $pool` for each pool
in the return.

=head1 SNMPD SETUP EXAMPLES

The extend name (C<zfs> below) is just a label LibreNMS uses
internally -- it can stay C<zfs> regardless of the script's on-disk
filename. Only the path needs to point at wherever C<zfs-stats> is
actually installed.

    extend zfs /etc/snmp/zfs-stats

=head1 REQUIREMENTS

The requirements may be installed via CPAN like below for Linux.

    apt-get install cpanminus File::Slurp MIME::Base64 JSON

Or on FreeBSD via pkg...

    pkg install p5-JSON p5-File-Slurp p5-MIME-Base64

=cut

